.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-SET-TOPIC-MAX-MESSAGE-LENGTH 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-max-message-length \- set the largest message a topic accepts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens set-topic-max-message-length
.BR \-\-topic | \-t
.I NAME
.BR \-\-max\-length | \-m
.I SIZE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the largest message a topic accepts.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-ens-create-topic (1)
takes this as
.BR \-\-max\-length ,
defaulting to 1M, and this is the only way to change it afterwards \- a
topic is otherwise created once and lives with the limit it was given.
.PP
A
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1)
whose body is larger is refused with HTTP 400, naming both the size sent
and the limit. The body alone is measured \- the same number
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-topic-metadata (1)
reports as
.IR size ,
so the limit is in the same units as the figures it is compared against.
Message attributes travel alongside and are not counted.
.PP
A topic that carries no limit of its own \- one created before the field
was sent, which stored a zero \- is measured against the 1M default
rather than refusing everything.
.SH WHEN IT TAKES EFFECT
For messages published afterwards. A message already in the topic was
accepted under the rule in force when it arrived, and lowering the limit
neither re-checks nor removes it.
.PP
That is deliberate, and the same rule
.BR euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-retention (1)
follows: what a topic holds is the record of what was published, and a
setting changed today is not a reason to lose what was accepted
yesterday. To empty a topic, use
.BR euclid-cli-ens-purge-topic (1).
.SH THE SIZE HAS TO BE POSITIVE
Zero is refused rather than read as "no limit": a topic that accepts
nothing is a mistake rather than a configuration. Taking nothing for a
while is
.BR euclid-cli-ens-stop-topic (1),
which says so reversibly and keeps what arrives meanwhile.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-topic " " \fINAME\fR
Topic name. A full ERN also works, and is what reaches another
namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-length " " \fISIZE\fR
The largest message to accept: a number of bytes, or a number with a
unit \- \fIb\fR, \fIk\fR, \fIm\fR or \fIg\fR, as in \fI512k\fR, \fI2M\fR
or \fI1G\fR. The units are binary, so \fI1M\fR is 1048576 bytes, which
is the number a topic starts out with. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The limit was set; the topic's ERN and its new limit are printed.
.TP
.B 1
No topic of that name, the size was zero or negative, or it was neither
a number of bytes nor a size this understands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Four megabytes, for a topic carrying rendered documents
euclid-cli ens set-topic-max-message-length --topic reports --max-length 4M

# The same thing in bytes
euclid-cli ens set-topic-max-message-length --topic reports --max-length 4194304

# Back to the size a topic is created with
euclid-cli ens set-topic-max-message-length --topic reports --max-length 1M
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-create-topic (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-retention (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-stop-topic (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-topic-metadata (1)
